  "status": "published",
  "title": "Floating Wall Shelf",
  "summary": "A clean red oak shelf that slides over a hidden 45-degree French cleat screwed into two studs. One afternoon, no visible brackets.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 3,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Rip a 3 1/2 in strip off the plywood, then rip that strip down the middle with the saw blade tilted to 45 degrees to make two matching cleat halves.",
    "Cut the oak into a 36 in top, a 36 in bottom and two 7 1/4 in end caps, and cut a 36 in front strip so the shelf becomes a hollow box open at the back.",
    "Glue one cleat half inside the box under the top, bevel facing down and toward the wall, then glue the box together and clamp it for an hour.",
    "Find two studs with the stud finder, hold the wall cleat level with the bevel facing up and toward the wall, and drive two 3 in screws into each stud.",
    "Sand the box from 120 to 220 grit, ease the edges, and wipe on three coats of polyurethane, letting each dry before the next.",
    "Lift the shelf onto the wall cleat so the two bevels lock together, and check it is level before loading it."
  ],
